### What is multi-level grouping?

Multi-level grouping means nesting one `groupingBy` as the downstream collector of another. The outer classifier partitions the stream into top-level groups, and the inner `groupingBy` partitions each of those groups again. The nesting can go as deep as you need.

Here department is the outer classifier and gender is the inner classifier.

```java
package com.bank.report;

import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class DiversityReport {
    public static Map<String, Map<String, List<Employee>>> byDeptThenGender(
            List<Employee> employees) {
        return employees.stream()
                .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                        Employee::getDepartment,
                        Collectors.groupingBy(Employee::getGender)));
    }
}
```

### How do you read the result type?

The result type reads directly off the nesting. The outer map is keyed by department `String`, its values are inner maps keyed by gender `String`, and those values are `List<Employee>`.

So `result.get("IT").get("M")` returns the list of male IT employees, and `result.get("IT").get("F")` returns the female IT employees.

```java
Map<String, Map<String, List<Employee>>> report = DiversityReport.byDeptThenGender(employees);

List<Employee> maleIt = report.get("IT").get("M");
List<Employee> femaleFinance = report.get("Finance").get("F");
```

### How does the nesting work internally?

Java first drops each employee into its department group using the outer classifier. The inner `groupingBy` is the downstream, so each department group is itself accumulated by another grouping collector that partitions by gender. The innermost values are collected into lists by default because `groupingBy` with no downstream implies `toList()`.

```java
package com.bank.report;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class Employee {
    private final String name;
    private final String department;
    private final String gender;
    private final BigDecimal salary;

    public Employee(String name, String department, String gender, BigDecimal salary) {
        this.name = name;
        this.department = department;
        this.gender = gender;
        this.salary = salary;
    }

    public String getName() { return name; }
    public String getDepartment() { return department; }
    public String getGender() { return gender; }
    public BigDecimal getSalary() { return salary; }

    @Override public String toString() { return name; }
}
```

### How do you run a full example?

```java
package com.bank.report;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

public class DiversityReportDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Employee> employees = List.of(
                new Employee("Rajesh", "IT", "M", new BigDecimal("1200000")),
                new Employee("Priya", "IT", "F", new BigDecimal("1100000")),
                new Employee("Amit", "IT", "M", new BigDecimal("900000")),
                new Employee("Pankaj", "Finance", "M", new BigDecimal("1500000")),
                new Employee("Neha", "Finance", "F", new BigDecimal("1300000")));

        Map<String, Map<String, List<Employee>>> report = employees.stream()
                .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                        Employee::getDepartment,
                        Collectors.groupingBy(Employee::getGender)));

        report.forEach((dept, byGender) -> {
            System.out.println(dept);
            byGender.forEach((gender, people) ->
                    System.out.println("  " + gender + " -> " + people));
        });
    }
}
```

```bash
javac com/bank/report/Employee.java com/bank/report/DiversityReportDemo.java
java com.bank.report.DiversityReportDemo
```

```text
Finance
  F -> [Neha]
  M -> [Pankaj]
IT
  F -> [Priya]
  M -> [Rajesh, Amit]
```

### How do you change the innermost collector?

The innermost collector is free. Instead of lists, you can count per department-and-gender with `counting()`, giving `Map<String, Map<String, Long>>`, which is a common shape for summary dashboards.

```java
Map<String, Map<String, Long>> counts = employees.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                Employee::getDepartment,
                Collectors.groupingBy(Employee::getGender,
                        Collectors.counting())));
```

```text
{Finance={F=1, M=1}, IT={F=1, M=2}}
```

### How is two-level grouping different from single-level grouping?

Single-level grouping gives one key per element value. Two-level grouping partitions each of those groups again, so the leaf collection holds only elements that match both classifiers.

| Aspect | Single-level groupingBy | Multi-level groupingBy |
|--------|-------------------------|------------------------|
| Collector form | `groupingBy(dept)` | `groupingBy(dept, groupingBy(gender))` |
| Result type | `Map<String, List<Employee>>` | `Map<String, Map<String, List<Employee>>>` |
| Access | `map.get("IT")` | `map.get("IT").get("M")` |
| Keys | one classifier value per key | pair of classifier values |

```java
// Single level: all IT employees together.
Map<String, List<Employee>> single = employees.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Employee::getDepartment));

// Two levels: IT split further by gender.
Map<String, Map<String, List<Employee>>> nested = employees.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(Employee::getDepartment,
                Collectors.groupingBy(Employee::getGender)));
```

### What are common mistakes?

A common mistake is reaching for a nested access key that does not exist, because the inner map only contains gender keys that actually occurred in that department. Another is assuming the maps are ordered.

```java
// WRONG: assumes every department has an "F" entry, may return null and NPE.
int count = report.get("IT").get("F").size(); // fine only if IT has a female employee

// CORRECT: guard against a missing inner key.
List<Employee> femaleIt = report
        .getOrDefault("IT", Map.of())
        .getOrDefault("F", List.of());
int safeCount = femaleIt.size();
```

### What are the edge cases and null handling?

Any classifier returning null at any level throws a `NullPointerException`. An empty stream yields an empty outer map. A department with employees of only one gender produces an inner map with a single key, not two.

```java
// Edge case: null gender crashes the inner grouping.
// Guard both classifiers when data may be dirty.
Map<String, Map<String, List<Employee>>> safe = employees.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                e -> e.getDepartment() == null ? "UNKNOWN_DEPT" : e.getDepartment(),
                Collectors.groupingBy(
                        e -> e.getGender() == null ? "U" : e.getGender())));
```

### How do you get ordered output with TreeMap?

Every level defaults to a `HashMap`, so iteration order is unspecified. Supply a `TreeMap` factory at each level with the four-argument `groupingBy` to get sorted departments and sorted genders.

```java
import java.util.TreeMap;

Map<String, Map<String, List<Employee>>> ordered = employees.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                Employee::getDepartment,
                TreeMap::new,
                Collectors.groupingBy(
                        Employee::getGender,
                        TreeMap::new,
                        Collectors.toList())));
```

```text
{Finance={F=[Neha], M=[Pankaj]}, IT={F=[Priya], M=[Rajesh, Amit]}}
```

### When should you use multi-level grouping, and when not?

Use multi-level grouping when a report genuinely has a hierarchy, like department then gender, or region then branch then product. Do not over-nest: if you only ever read by a combined key, a single `groupingBy` on a composite key record can be simpler to consume.

```java
// Alternative: group by a composite key record instead of nesting.
record DeptGender(String department, String gender) {}

Map<DeptGender, List<Employee>> flat = employees.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                e -> new DeptGender(e.getDepartment(), e.getGender())));
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Interviewers ask how the downstream collector enables nesting, how deep you can nest, how to count at the leaf, how to order the maps, and when a composite key is preferable to a nested map. A senior answer notes that each level is an independent collector, so you can mix levels, for instance group by department then reduce to the average salary by gender inside.

```java
import java.util.DoubleSummaryStatistics;

// Follow-up: department -> gender -> average salary (non-money demo value).
Map<String, Map<String, Double>> avgByDeptGender = employees.stream()
        .collect(Collectors.groupingBy(
                Employee::getDepartment,
                Collectors.groupingBy(
                        Employee::getGender,
                        Collectors.averagingDouble(e -> e.getSalary().doubleValue()))));
```
